  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThrough next week (Friday)...\n\nThis weekend: GOES East water vapor and RAP analysis indicate cold \ncore upper low over Lake Erie and trough axis down into the OH \nValley into Appalachians and an upper low over the Baja \nCalifornia into Desert Southwest. At the surface, reinforcing cold\nfront has moved to the southeast of the area. Cooler and drier \nair will dominate this pattern. Increased isentropic ascent and \ncirrus are spreading into the area this morning, with mid to high \nlevel cloudiness persisting through the weekend. 1025mb surface \nhigh will build in late weekend into the Mid South, bringing much \ncooler temperatures today and Sunday (upper 60s northwest of the \nNatchez Trace of Interstate 20 while upper 60s to low 70s to the \nsoutheast while low to mid 70s Sunday). Seasonable lows are \nexpected this morning while seasonably cool this weekend (40F to \n52F this morning and 33F to 43F Saturday and Sunday nights). \nPatchy frost remains a concern this weekend. There will be some \ngradient wind this morning and again Saturday night, but become \ncalm Sunday night. The dry soil conditions will support \noverachieving of the nocturnal cooling processes. At the same \ntime, we'll have to consider the potential for cirrus cloud decks,\nwhich could keep temperatures from dropping as much. Putting \nthese factors together yields uncertainty for low temperatures and\npotential frost impacts. Regardless, frost potential remains a \nconcern, especially in low- lying and sheltered areas. Ensure \nplants are covered or indoors and consideration for pets and \npeople cold temperatures with frost to light freeze potential \npersists. Kept inherited Limited HWO graphic with minimal \nadjustments for patchy frost during these nights, mainly in north-\ncentral, east to northeast MS.\n\nEarly to mid next week: Quiet, cloudy and mostly dry pattern will \npersist into the following week. Moist, isentropic ascent and cloudy \nconditions will remain draped across the Gulf Coast states through \nat least mid week (Wednesday). Isolated rain shower is possible \naround midweek, but probs remain isolated and low (less than 15 \npercent). Upper low is still projected to become open shortwave, \nascent will be less prominent as it propagates eastward. With some \nthermal temps warming, highs will moderate to seasonably through mid \nweek (69F to 78F Monday to 73F to 80F by Wednesday). Lows will \nremain seasonable. The synoptic pattern has come a little more in \nline than yesterday for late week: deep synoptic trough with main \ndiscrepancies on western longitude of spokes of energy ejecting \nsoutheast out of the Plains from the GFS while the Euro shows mean \ntrough axis diving east of the Plains through the MS River Valley. \nCluster analysis indicates low to medium percentage of members (20 \nto 40 percent) from the ECENS and GEFS. This continues to favor \nclusters/pattern closer to the operational Euro, GEFS and AI (global \nmodel suite and ensemble). Recent GFS has begun to trend this \ndirection. This favors a drier and cooler pattern into late week \ninto next weekend and another round of patchy frost or light freeze. \nThe synoptic setup and temperatures remain  a potential target of \nopportunity. Will have to monitor the synoptic setup for \nincreased wildfire danger as gradient winds could increase in a \nwell-mixed dry boundary layer by Friday. /DC/EC/",
